# Independent review: authorized attempt submission ordering

Verdict: no blocking finding in the reviewed intent/POST/receipt ordering layer. The implementation diff was reviewed against base eaca21b; exact final snapshots are under source/.

Reviewed submit.py, five real-Git tests, README and the reused create/journal/remote boundaries against chapter 15's durable intent before API, single attempt, unresolved intent, receipt preservation and transaction budget requirements. The adapter validates request/intent data and expected local/remote heads, rejects reuse of the transaction/kind pair, writes identity-only intent evidence, normally pushes and confirms it before the sole create call, then validates/persists the raw receipt and confirms its remote head. Credentials do not enter journal evidence. A completed receipt is retained even if the final budget check prevents a successful return.

Independent execution: five real-Git tests passed normally and with Python -O. These verify server-side intent/evidence before create, one call and remote receipt on success, lost response retaining an intent and blocking replay, failed intent push preventing create, RecoveryReceipt retention after failed receipt push, and expiration after intent confirmation preventing create. Additional probe.py independently checks a failed DeployReceipt push with local raw receipt/remote intent preservation and replay rejection, and an actual RecoveryReceipt push followed by simulated budget expiration: both journals retain the raw receipt, but the adapter raises CreationUnknown instead of returning success. commands.json/raw test logs and probe-results.json preserve evidence.

The API send is replaced with synthetic responses or errors in these tests; all Git servers are temporary local bare repositories. No real Pages creation, credentials, authenticated remote write or publisher authorization check was performed. The second-push failure is deliberately injected before that push rather than proving every possible acknowledgement-loss timing.

The README accurately requires prior eligibility, provenance, current-state reconciliation, lock and protected-remote checks. The Event alone does not establish them. Reuse of a different transaction ID is technically possible and explicitly remains forbidden as a way to bypass publisher reconciliation; this module does not implement that state machine. Git operations can overrun a wall-clock deadline, but no create begins after the post-intent budget check is exhausted, and late receipt completion cannot report success. Public smoke, recovery eligibility and LKG promotion remain separate. No production edits were made by the reviewer.
